WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000
Reviewer: Denise RQ

00:00:16.326 --> 00:00:20.636
War is a nightmare.

00:00:21.985 --> 00:00:23.655
War is awful.

00:00:24.765 --> 00:00:29.945
It is indifferent,
and devastating, and evil.

00:00:32.326 --> 00:00:34.936
War is hell.

00:00:39.314 --> 00:00:43.546
But war is also an incredible teacher,

00:00:44.746 --> 00:00:46.686
a brutal teacher.

00:00:47.506 --> 00:00:51.636
And it teaches you lessons
that you will not forget.

00:00:55.624 --> 00:00:58.833
In war, you are forced

00:00:58.834 --> 00:01:04.114
to see humanity at its absolute worst,

00:01:06.074 --> 00:01:08.540
and you are also blessed

00:01:09.463 --> 00:01:14.013
to see humanity
in its most glorious moments.

00:01:16.543 --> 00:01:22.833
War teaches you about
sorrow, and loss, and pain.

00:01:25.126 --> 00:01:28.156
And it teaches you about the preciousness

00:01:29.479 --> 00:01:33.249
and the fragility of human life.

00:01:35.229 --> 00:01:37.509
And in that fragility,

00:01:39.132 --> 00:01:41.602
war teaches you about death.

00:01:45.972 --> 00:01:48.693
But war also teaches you

00:01:49.477 --> 00:01:53.157
about brotherhood, and honor,

00:01:53.783 --> 00:01:57.433
and humility, and leadership.

00:02:00.013 --> 00:02:05.663
And unfortunately, war teaches you
the most when things go wrong.

00:02:08.469 --> 00:02:09.967
And for me,

00:02:09.969 --> 00:02:13.648
one of the most impactful lessons
that I learned from war

00:02:14.358 --> 00:02:17.648
was in the spring of 2006

00:02:18.679 --> 00:02:21.138
in the city of Ramadi, Iraq,

00:02:21.139 --> 00:02:25.349
which at the time,
was the epicenter of the insurgency

00:02:26.443 --> 00:02:31.253
where brutal and determined terrorists
ruled the streets

00:02:32.326 --> 00:02:38.456
with torture and rape and murder.

00:02:41.042 --> 00:02:44.152
It was in one neighborhood of that city

00:02:45.095 --> 00:02:48.165
during an operation
that I was in charge of

00:02:49.205 --> 00:02:51.735
when all hell broke loose.

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:57.709
We had multiple units out
on the battlefield fighting the enemy.

00:02:57.710 --> 00:02:59.879
We had friendly Iraqi soldiers,

00:03:00.299 --> 00:03:03.091
we had US Army soldiers and US Marines,

00:03:03.092 --> 00:03:06.482
along with small elements of my SEAL team.

00:03:08.585 --> 00:03:11.805
And then the fog of war rolled in

00:03:13.460 --> 00:03:17.000
with its confusion, and chaos, and mayhem,

00:03:19.489 --> 00:03:23.259
and with its gunfire, and enemy attacks,

00:03:23.757 --> 00:03:27.887
and screaming men, and blood, and death;

00:03:31.209 --> 00:03:34.309
and in that fog of war,

00:03:36.223 --> 00:03:40.763
through a series of mistakes,
and human error,

00:03:40.771 --> 00:03:45.601
and poor judgment, and Murphy's law,
and just plain bad luck,

00:03:46.562 --> 00:03:50.132
a horrendous fire fight broke out.

00:03:51.938 --> 00:03:54.088
But this fire fight

00:03:55.417 --> 00:03:59.007
wasn't between us and the enemy;

00:04:00.472 --> 00:04:03.492
this fire fight, tragically,

00:04:04.031 --> 00:04:08.651
was between us and us,

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:13.629
friendly forces against friendly forces;

00:04:13.630 --> 00:04:15.380
fratricide -

00:04:16.072 --> 00:04:18.973
the mortal sin of combat

00:04:19.437 --> 00:04:22.927
and the most horrific part of war.

00:04:25.849 --> 00:04:31.009
And when it was over,
and the fog of war lifted,

00:04:31.816 --> 00:04:37.946
one friendly Iraqi soldier was dead,
two more were wounded,

00:04:38.398 --> 00:04:40.797
one of my men was wounded,

00:04:40.798 --> 00:04:43.268
the rest of my SEALs were badly shaken,

00:04:43.279 --> 00:04:46.559
and it was only through a miracle
that no one else was killed.

00:04:49.013 --> 00:04:52.993
And it was reported up the chain
of command what had happened.

00:04:54.526 --> 00:04:59.656
That we had fought, and wounded,
and killed each other.

00:05:03.153 --> 00:05:06.943
And when we got back to base,
things didn't get much better.

00:05:07.512 --> 00:05:11.742
There was a message waiting for me
from my commanding officer,

00:05:13.260 --> 00:05:16.520
and it said, "Shut down all operations."

00:05:17.776 --> 00:05:19.635
It said that the commanding officer,

00:05:19.636 --> 00:05:22.625
the master chief,
and the investigating officer

00:05:22.626 --> 00:05:25.246
were inbound to my location.

00:05:26.750 --> 00:05:31.488
And they told me to prepare
a debrief to explain

00:05:32.168 --> 00:05:37.378
exactly what had happened on the operation
and what had gone wrong.

00:05:41.426 --> 00:05:44.256
Now, I knew what this meant.

00:05:46.143 --> 00:05:47.772
It meant that somebody had to pay.

00:05:47.773 --> 00:05:51.122
It meant that somebody
had to be held accountable.

00:05:51.123 --> 00:05:54.913
It meant that somebody had to get fired
for what had happened.

00:05:57.077 --> 00:06:01.317
So, I began to prepare my debrief.

00:06:02.719 --> 00:06:03.969
And in it,

00:06:04.474 --> 00:06:10.384
I detailed every mistake
that was made and who made it.

00:06:11.260 --> 00:06:14.039
And I pointed out every failure

00:06:14.040 --> 00:06:19.220
in the planning, and the preparation,
and the execution in the operation,

00:06:19.750 --> 00:06:24.350
and I pointed out
who was responsible for that failure.

00:06:25.906 --> 00:06:29.486
There was plenty of blame to go around.

00:06:30.303 --> 00:06:35.203
There were so many people
that I could incriminate with guilt.

00:06:38.410 --> 00:06:41.340
But something wasn't right.

00:06:42.427 --> 00:06:47.947
For some reason,
I just couldn't put my finger

00:06:48.466 --> 00:06:51.005
on who was at fault

00:06:51.006 --> 00:06:55.176
and who specifically I should blame for
what had happened.

00:06:57.075 --> 00:06:58.825
And I sat,

00:06:59.507 --> 00:07:04.457
and I went over it again and again,
and I struggled for an answer.

00:07:06.804 --> 00:07:11.244
And then, when I was about ten minutes
from starting the debrief,

00:07:11.934 --> 00:07:13.894
that answer came,

00:07:14.173 --> 00:07:17.023
and it hit me like a slap in the face.

00:07:17.801 --> 00:07:19.150
And I realized

00:07:19.151 --> 00:07:22.690
that there was only one person
to blame for the confusion,

00:07:22.691 --> 00:07:26.210
only one person to blame for
the wounded men,

00:07:26.211 --> 00:07:30.011
and only one person to blame for
the dead Iraqi soldier.

00:07:30.518 --> 00:07:32.918
And I knew exactly who that person was.

00:07:34.487 --> 00:07:38.387
And with that knowledge,
I walked into the debriefing room

00:07:38.791 --> 00:07:40.630
with my commanding officer

00:07:40.631 --> 00:07:43.530
and the master chief,
and the investigating officer

00:07:43.531 --> 00:07:47.340
were sitting there waiting for me,
along with the rest of my men,

00:07:47.341 --> 00:07:49.310
including my SEAL who had been wounded,

00:07:49.311 --> 00:07:51.544
who was sitting in the back of the room

00:07:51.545 --> 00:07:54.345
with his head and his face
all bandaged up.

00:07:56.421 --> 00:08:00.971
And I stood up before them,
and I asked them one simple question,

00:08:02.711 --> 00:08:05.381
"Who's fault was this?"

00:08:08.225 --> 00:08:10.025
One of my SEALs raised his hand.

00:08:10.457 --> 00:08:12.207
And he said, "It was my fault.

00:08:12.697 --> 00:08:16.607
I didn't keep control of
the Iraqi soldiers I was with.

00:08:16.891 --> 00:08:19.180
And they left their designated sector,

00:08:19.181 --> 00:08:21.991
and that was the root
of all these problems."

00:08:23.026 --> 00:08:25.085
And I said, "No, it wasn't your fault."

00:08:25.086 --> 00:08:28.300
And then another SEAL raised his hand
and said, "It was my fault.

00:08:29.280 --> 00:08:33.188
I didn't pass our location
over the radio fast enough

00:08:33.190 --> 00:08:37.111
so no one knew what building we were in,
and that's what caused all this confusion.

00:08:37.113 --> 00:08:38.363
It was my fault."

00:08:39.172 --> 00:08:41.792
And I said, "No, it wasn't
your fault either."

00:08:43.135 --> 00:08:46.504
And then another SEAL
raised his hand, and he said,

00:08:46.505 --> 00:08:49.615
"Boss, this was my fault.

00:08:50.828 --> 00:08:54.128
I didn't properly identify my target,

00:08:55.521 --> 00:08:59.071
and I shot and killed
that friendly Iraqi soldier.

00:09:00.053 --> 00:09:01.152
This was my fault."

00:09:01.153 --> 00:09:04.353
And I said, "No, this wasn't
your fault either.

00:09:04.837 --> 00:09:07.616
And it wasn't yours, or yours, or yours,"

00:09:07.617 --> 00:09:10.517
I said as I pointed to the rest
of the SEALs in the room.

00:09:11.927 --> 00:09:13.446
And then I told them

00:09:13.447 --> 00:09:17.756
that there was only one person
at fault for what had happened.

00:09:17.757 --> 00:09:20.085
There was only one person to blame.

00:09:22.315 --> 00:09:24.895
And that person was me.

00:09:26.449 --> 00:09:27.959
I am the commander,

00:09:28.774 --> 00:09:31.183
I am the senior man on the battlefield,

00:09:31.184 --> 00:09:35.184
and I am responsible for
everything that happens;

00:09:36.764 --> 00:09:38.454
everything.

00:09:40.881 --> 00:09:43.741
And then I went on to explain to them

00:09:43.763 --> 00:09:46.863
some new tactics,
techniques, and procedures

00:09:46.873 --> 00:09:48.793
that we were going to implement

00:09:48.794 --> 00:09:52.574
to ensure that this kind of travesty
never happened again.

00:09:55.789 --> 00:09:58.839
And I will tell you something: it hurt.

00:10:00.303 --> 00:10:02.602
It hurt my ego.

00:10:02.603 --> 00:10:05.504
It hurt my pride to take the blame.

00:10:07.233 --> 00:10:09.323
But I also knew,

00:10:10.264 --> 00:10:17.264
I knew that to maintain my integrity
as a leader and as a man,

00:10:19.186 --> 00:10:21.045
I had to take responsibility.

00:10:21.046 --> 00:10:22.235
And in order to do that,

00:10:22.236 --> 00:10:27.236
I had to control my ego
so that my ego did not control me.

00:10:29.939 --> 00:10:31.459
And you know what?

00:10:32.723 --> 00:10:33.943
I didn't get fired.

00:10:35.419 --> 00:10:38.759
In fact, my commanding officer,

00:10:39.619 --> 00:10:43.619
who had expected excuses
and finger-pointing,

00:10:44.237 --> 00:10:47.376
when I took responsibility,
when I took ownership,

00:10:47.377 --> 00:10:50.467
he now trusted me even more.

00:10:52.937 --> 00:10:54.477
And my men

00:10:56.406 --> 00:10:59.176
didn't lose respect for me.

00:11:00.947 --> 00:11:03.537
Instead, they realized

00:11:03.719 --> 00:11:07.719
that I would never shirk responsibility,

00:11:08.180 --> 00:11:10.549
and I would never pass

00:11:10.550 --> 00:11:16.210
that heavy burden of command
down the chain and onto them.

00:11:19.666 --> 00:11:21.266
And you know what?

00:11:21.589 --> 00:11:23.899
They had the same attitude.

00:11:25.197 --> 00:11:31.116
Unlike a team where no one
takes ownership of the problems,

00:11:31.117 --> 00:11:33.826
and therefore, the problems
never get solved,

00:11:33.827 --> 00:11:38.176
with us, everyone took ownership
of their mistakes,

00:11:38.177 --> 00:11:40.994
everyone took ownership of the problems.

00:11:42.344 --> 00:11:46.583
And when a team
takes ownership of it's problems,

00:11:46.584 --> 00:11:49.334
the problems get solved.

00:11:51.745 --> 00:11:55.745
And that is true on the battlefield,

00:11:56.051 --> 00:12:00.161
it is true in business,
and it is true in life.

00:12:03.893 --> 00:12:08.942
So I say, take ownership;

00:12:08.943 --> 00:12:11.495
take extreme ownership.

00:12:11.854 --> 00:12:13.423
Don't make excuses,

00:12:13.424 --> 00:12:17.424
don't blame any other person
or any other thing.

00:12:20.026 --> 00:12:22.526
Get control of your ego.

00:12:23.604 --> 00:12:29.984
Don't hide your delicate pride
from the truth.

00:12:31.774 --> 00:12:35.493
Take ownership
of everything in your world,

00:12:35.494 --> 00:12:37.404
the good and the bad.

00:12:39.316 --> 00:12:44.395
Take ownership of your mistakes,
take ownership of your shortfalls,

00:12:44.396 --> 00:12:46.125
take ownership of your problems,

00:12:46.126 --> 00:12:51.866
and then take ownership of the solutions
that will get those problems solved.

00:12:55.570 --> 00:12:59.480
Take ownership of your mission.

00:13:00.035 --> 00:13:04.824
Take ownership of your job,
of your team, of your future,

00:13:04.825 --> 00:13:08.055
and take ownership of your life.

00:13:12.418 --> 00:13:13.788
And lead.

00:13:16.098 --> 00:13:17.258
Lead.

00:13:19.938 --> 00:13:22.018
Lead yourself,

00:13:22.766 --> 00:13:27.155
and your team,
and the people in your life;

00:13:27.156 --> 00:13:29.034
lead them all

00:13:31.690 --> 00:13:33.150
to victory.

00:13:35.270 --> 00:13:36.379
Thank you.

00:13:36.380 --> 00:13:37.550
(Applause)

